# R5 — Inspector per boundary

When you add a new cross-package boundary, or change the shape of an
existing one, a corresponding inspector page in `apps/inspector/` **must**
be added or updated **in the same change**. The inspector is part of
"done", not a follow-up.

This is the rule that prevents us from re-living The Body Field — where
debug UI was bolted on after the fact, lagged behind reality, and ended
up cluttering the main app. Here the inspector *is* how we know it works.

## Boundary → inspector page mapping

| Boundary | Inspector page |
|---|---|
| `LLMClient` (prompt → tool calls) | `01-prompt-to-tool-calls` |
| `WorldMemoryStore` (memory-graph) | `02-world-state-graph` |
| `WorldAPIClient` (world-api) | `03-world-api-mock-bench` |
| `RCBridge` (rc-bridge) | `04-rc-round-trip` |
| `PCGRunner` (pcg) | `05-pcg-inspector` |
| `StreamingMetrics` (streaming) | `06-streaming-diagnostics` |
| Cross-cutting trace | `07-pipeline-trace-viewer` |

## Checklist when touching a boundary

- [ ] Updated the interface / Protocol (R2)
- [ ] At least one mock impl reflects the new shape (R1, R2)
- [ ] All boundary methods are `@boundary`-wrapped (R3)
- [ ] Contract types come from the canonical package only (R4)
- [ ] **Inspector page** for this boundary renders the new inputs/outputs
- [ ] Inspector still works against the **mock** impl with no real backend

## ❌ BAD

> "I'll add a new `getWeatherHistory` method to `WorldAPIClient` now and
> update the inspector in a follow-up."

This is exactly what we are ruling out. The inspector update is part of the
same change.

## ✅ GOOD

One change adds the interface method, the mock impl, the real impl
stub, and renders the new I/O in the inspector page.
